  1598                              <1> ; =============================================================================
  1599                              <1> ;  The line editor (EDITOR = 1)
  1600                              <1> ;
  1601                              <1> ;  The interpreter starts here, and a program that ends comes back here.
  1602                              <1> ;  There is no prompt. It follows the spec's suggested standard for a line
  1603                              <1> ;  editor (docs/b8script.md, Environments), and is the Z80's editor, key for
  1604                              <1> ;  key. Type a line and press Return:
  1605                              <1> ;
  1606                              <1> ;    n text    put the text in as line n; the lines from n on move down. A
  1607                              <1> ;              number past the end (or 0) adds the line at the end.
  1608                              <1> ;    n         take line n out
  1609                              <1> ;    anything else is run at once, as B8Script. (Start such a line with a
  1610                              <1> ;    space if it would begin with a digit.)
  1611                              <1> ;
  1612                              <1> ;  A command key ends the line too, and takes what was typed as its argument:
  1613                              <1> ;
  1614                              <1> ;    ^G        run the program
  1615                              <1> ;    n ^L      list from line n (no number: all of it). Any key holds the
  1616                              <1> ;              list until another key is pressed; Ctrl-C ends it.
  1617                              <1> ;    n ^E      put line n in the input line; Return puts it back, changed
  1618                              <1> ;    name ^R   read the file of that name (no name: the current one: the
  1619                              <1> ;              one from the command line, or the last one typed)
  1620                              <1> ;    name ^W   write the program to that file (no name: the current one)
  1621                              <1> ;    ^N        new: no program
  1622                              <1> ;    ^X        leave
  1623                              <1> ;
  1624                              <1> ;  Any other control key does nothing.
  1625                              <1> ;
  1626                              <1> ;  In the line: the left and right arrow keys move the cursor (^B and ^F do
  1627                              <1> ;  nothing);
  1628                              <1> ;  a character goes in at the cursor; Backspace and DEL take out the one
  1629                              <1> ;  before it; Esc drops the line (after Esc it waits 5 ms for a '[': Esc [ C
  1630                              <1> ;  and Esc [ D are right and left too). Only CR, LF, BS and printing
  1631                              <1> ;  characters are sent to the terminal.
  1632                              <1> ;
  1633                              <1> ;  Lines are counted, not numbered. A typed line holds 125 characters; the
  1634                              <1> ;  one character after a line number separates it from the text and is not
  1635                              <1> ;  stored. The text is kept as typed, each line ending in CR LF, at PROG. A
  1636                              <1> ;  file is read up to its first ^Z, and a last line with no line end is
  1637                              <1> ;  given one; it is written as it is, with no padding.
  1638                              <1> ;
  1639                              <1> ;  What lasts from one line to the next: the program, the words, the
  1640                              <1> ;  variables and the data stack (TOS is kept pushed while the editor runs).
  1641                              <1> ;  An error or a break clears both stacks. The return stack starts empty for
  1642                              <1> ;  every line.
  1643                              <1> ;
  1644                              <1> ;  Registers in the editor: EBX = the cursor, EDX = the end of the line
  1645                              <1> ;  (pointers into the line buffer); EDI is free (edit resets it).
  1646                              <1> ; =============================================================================
  1647                              <1> %if EDITOR
  1648                              <1> ; ^E: line n goes into the input line, to be changed. V_EDLN remembers
  1649                              <1> ; which line it was: Return puts the text back in its place.
  1650                              <1> cedln:  CALLP   rdnum
  1650                              <2> cedln: 
